# Development happens on Windows with core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites line endings on
# checkout. That is harmless for source, and not harmless for files whose exact bytes matter.
#
# A `.nedito` file is written by encodeDoc() with LF endings, and examples/ has a test that
# re-encodes the committed document and asserts the bytes match. Left to autocrlf, a fresh
# clone would check that file out with CRLF and the test would fail for everyone except
# whoever generated it — the classic "passes on my machine" failure.
*.nedito text eol=lf
# The same for documents written before the rename (D100), kept as fixtures.
*.deepdraw text eol=lf

# Same reasoning for the golden files the exporters are checked against, and for anything
# whose bytes are compared rather than parsed.
*.svg text eol=lf

# Never touched: these are containers whose CRC/offsets a line-ending rewrite would corrupt.
*.png binary
*.pptx binary
*.docx binary
*.pdf binary
*.woff2 binary
